A||||11||85fierogt||rear brake calipers||||1||
Z||000000||85fierogt||10-24-2001||09:04 PM||||okey dokey guys yet another question, ok im replacing all my brake pads on my restoration of an 85 GT 2.8l v6 and well i know its not frozen but i cant get that piston thing to push back, i tried the C-clamp method, do we need that special tool, or is ther another way, all info would be greatly appreciated, thanks<BR>-brad||||reg||1
Z||000001||varnster||10-24-2001||09:28 PM||||I think the only diff in brakes are the 88. I had  the dust seal on the piston get screwy on one<BR>of my rear calipers when I did my change (87), if you can just get it to push back enough to clear the rotor then you can give it a good inspection, it should push in easy enough unless their is a problem with the caliper. make sure you are pushing nice and even on the piston and not cocking it to one side.||||reg||1
Z||000002||samoht||10-24-2001||09:33 PM||||i am not sure what you are asking, but if you take off the E-brake arm while it is on the car you should be able to screw it out with the E-brake attachment nut, or at least you can compress the piston then.. Hope that helps.. maybe the last person ajusted the ebrake out and now you need to adjust it back out to aquire the new pad clearance<P>85gt3.2||||reg||1
Z||000003||MrPBody||10-24-2001||09:40 PM||||Have you read Ogre's procedure?  The C-clamp and socket worked for me.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Hippocrat||10-24-2001||10:21 PM||||Heres how I do it. <P>I just did mine on the weekend. I used a pair of needle nose vice grips and clamped on to the head of the piston and turned it back in. Being careful not to damage the rubber seal.<P>Point of note that I learned in the past. The drivers side and passenger side turn  in opposite directions. Turn it too far in the wrong direction and man what a job to get the piston back in past the ring when it pops out.<P>I did however get a good look at how these things work thanks to that little mistake last year.<P>Just my two cents.<p>[This message has been edited by Hippocrat (edited 10-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||10-25-2001||07:23 PM||||84-87 calipers with the plastic "plug" in the piston face should not be rotated. This is per GM's instructions. The clamp method needs to be used to prevent piston damage.<P>the C clamp method requires removal of the e brake arms and the e brake screw must Not be frozen in the caliper...<P>I am still trying to find data on the solid face pistons. The only one I have is good so I'm not going to risk trashing it to find out how it's built. These may not care as much about rotating them in. There's nothing I can find from GM or anyone else about them so far.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000006||FTF Engineering||10-26-2001||10:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>I am still trying to find data on the solid face pistons. The only one I have is good so I'm not going to risk trashing it to find out how it's built. These may not care as much about rotating them in. There's nothing I can find from GM or anyone else about them so far.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey Ogre, I can tell you anything you want to know about the solid face pistons including the little detail that they aren't as solid as you might think...<P>Take a <B>good</B> look at the side of the piston just behind the dust seal. You will find the hole. All they did was make it smaller and move it from the center of the face to the radius.<P>IMHO that was a step in the wrong direction. It doesn't prevent the leak problem... All it does is delay your noticing it because it's hidden behind the dust seal.<P>The guts inside are exactly the same as the original pistons with the exception that they used a better bearing on the adjuster mechanism (new bearing has more balls than the original design and does a better job of distributing the load). Other than that, no difference...<P>Anything else you want to know?<P>-Bruce at FTF Engineering||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||10-27-2001||12:50 PM||||Special... Thanks... That means that these shouldn't be rotated either. (I'll add your info to the brake service post as soon as I get a chance. Then it won't get burried...)<P>No, I don't like that they simply moved the vent either. You could look at it as a typical GM cost cutting measure but it also sovles problems of people using various squeal stop adhesives and blocking up the face vent. (Most people, including many mechanics, don't realize the plug isn't actually solid...)<P>As long as the dust seal is in good shape, the side opening shouldn't be any more of a problem in terms of stuff getting into the piston. I do agree is makes it harder to detect the failure of the piston's internal seal.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Seanh||10-27-2001||05:42 PM||||What is the point of the little rubber vent/valve in the center of the piston anyways? I recall replacing it when I rebuilt the caliper, but have no idea what its for and I do use squeal preventer.||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||10-28-2001||01:00 PM||||The "plug" is actually an air vent. This is explaned more in the brake service article in my cave.<P>Squeal prevention adhesives are smeared on the back of a brake pad to reduce/stop pad squeal. It should NEVER be used on the inner pad when the piston has the "plugged face." The adhesive will seal the plug and cause brake problems.||||reg||1
Z||000010||FTF Engineering||10-28-2001||06:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>Special... Thanks... That means that these shouldn't be rotated either.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ogre, I can tell you that rotating the piston shouldn't hurt any of the guts on it's insides. As a matter of fact, the guts are SUPPOSED to rotate by design. That's how the rear pads are adjusted. The only differences are:<P>a) they normally rotate a little bit every time you release the parking brake instead <BR>of all at once when you spin the piston, and...<P>b) they normally spin the guts and hold the piston body stationary as opposed to spinning the piston body an holding the guts stationary.<P>I wonder if GM is simply worried about tearing the dust seal...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><P>-Bruce at FTF Engineering||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||10-28-2001||09:29 PM||||I'm not sure what GM's logic is. I can think of a couple posibilities but can't be certain.<P>I've tried both methods. I thought it took way more force to rotate a Fiero caliper than most of the others I've done. Most rotate fairly easy. That set off alarm bells and sent me looking for the "official" method.<P>Even if I was totally certain it wouldn't harm the caliper I'll continue to use the clamp. That method works well and also forces people to check over the actuator screw and it's outer seal. It also works better when the holes in the piston face are worn because people ran them without the pad clip on them... When that happens the clamp or big pliers are your choices and pliers can really chew up the pistons.||||reg||1
